                                    <h1>YouTube Ads Cost for D2C Brands 2026-2027 (India + Global)</h1>
                                    <p>YouTube is one of the most under-invested channels for D2C brands — high intent, long-form content trust, sub-1s Shorts formats, and Performance Max integration all matter. Here are real 2026-2027 benchmarks for YouTube ad costs across India, UAE, USA, UK, Australia.</p>
                                    <h2>YouTube CPM by Format + Industry (India 2026)</h2>
                                    <p><strong>TrueView In-stream (skippable) — India:</strong> ₹80-280 CPM (charge only on 30s+ view).<br><strong>YouTube Shorts ads — India:</strong> ₹40-180 CPM (Reels-competitive pricing).<br><strong>Bumper ads (6s):</strong> ₹150-450 CPM.<br><strong>Masthead:</strong> ₹15-50 lakh per day (enterprise only).<br><strong>Performance Max (YouTube inventory):</strong> included in blended PMax bidding.</p>
                                    <h2>CPV (Cost Per View) — TrueView Global</h2>
                                    <p><strong>India:</strong> ₹0.50-3.<br><strong>UAE:</strong> AED 0.15-0.80.<br><strong>USA:</strong> $0.08-0.45.<br><strong>UK:</strong> £0.06-0.35.<br><strong>Australia:</strong> AUD 0.10-0.50.<br>Charge only when viewer watches 30+ seconds — inherently qualified engagement.</p>
                                    <h2>YouTube Shorts vs TrueView</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Shorts:</strong> lower CPM, higher volume, younger audience skew. Best for brand awareness + Gen-Z targeting.<br><strong>TrueView In-stream:</strong> higher CPM but qualified engagement — better for consideration + high-consideration categories.<br><strong>Recommendation:</strong> Shorts for prospecting + TrueView for consideration + Discovery ads for high-intent search-adjacent.</p>
                                    <h2>ROAS Benchmarks</h2>
                                    <p>YouTube direct-response ROAS is harder to measure than Meta / Google Search due to view-through attribution complexity.<br><strong>Best proxies:</strong> brand-search lift post-YouTube campaigns (25-50% common), YouTube-attributed conversions (in GA4), incrementality testing.<br><strong>Direct ROAS (last-click):</strong> 1.2-3x typical, understates true impact.<br><strong>Blended ROAS with proper attribution:</strong> 2-5x for consideration categories.</p>
                                    <h2>Category-Specific YouTube Playbook</h2>
                                    <p><strong>Beauty:</strong> tutorial + ingredient education content. Highest fit.<br><strong>Wellness / supplements:</strong> long-form science content. Very high fit.<br><strong>Consumer electronics:</strong> unboxing + comparison + review content.<br><strong>Fashion:</strong> styling + trend content.<br><strong>Jewelry:</strong> heritage + storytelling + craftsmanship content.<br><strong>F&amp;B:</strong> recipe + ritual + origin content.<br><strong>Home:</strong> transformation + tutorial content.</p>
                                    <h2>What Actually Works on YouTube</h2>
                                    <p>1. <strong>Creator content amplified via paid</strong> — best CPM efficiency + native feel.<br>2. <strong>Long-form content (3-15 min) for consideration categories.</strong><br>3. <strong>Shorts for prospecting + brand awareness.</strong><br>4. <strong>YouTube Search ads</strong> (Discovery format) for high-intent queries.<br>5. <strong>Sequential retargeting</strong> — different creative at different funnel stages.<br>6. <strong>Performance Max</strong> integration for cross-format optimisation.</p>

